{"loc":"RAZI-FULL:v23p67","rows":[{"verses":["23:1"],"words":["قَدْ أَفْلَحَ ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ"],"type":"interpretation","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"God promises success to those who combine seven qualities; the segment begins explaining those qualities with “the believers.”","anchor":"حكم بحصول الفلاح لمن كان مستجمعا لصفات سبع","mentions":[]},{"verses":["23:1"],"words":["قَدْ"],"type":"rhetoric","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"“Qad” contrasts with “lammā”: it affirms an expected event, here the believers’ anticipated glad tidings that success is firmly theirs.","anchor":"فقد تثبت المتوقع ولما تنفيه ولا شك أن المؤمنين كانوا متوقعين لمثل هذه البشارة","mentions":[]},{"verses":["23:1"],"words":["أَفْلَحَ"],"type":"meaning","speaker":"author","stance":"reports","claim":"Falāḥ means attaining one’s desired end, or remaining in good; aflaha can mean entering success, as abshara means entering glad tidings, or it can be causative, “he made him succeed.”","anchor":"الفلاح الظفر بالراد وقيل البقاء في الخير وأفلح دخل في الفلاح كأبشر دخل في البشارة","mentions":[]},{"verses":["23:1"],"words":["أَفْلَحَ"],"type":"readings","speaker":"طلحة بن مصرف","stance":"reports","claim":"Talha ibn Musarrif read aflaha in the passive; a further form aflaha is attributed to him either by the “akalūnī al-barāghīth” dialect or by an indefinite verb followed by an explanatory subject.","anchor":"وعليه قراءة طلحة بن مصرف أفلح على البناء للمفعول وعنه أفلحوا على لغة أكلوني البراغيث","mentions":[]},{"verses":["23:1"],"words":["ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ"],"type":"links","speaker":"author","stance":"holds","claim":"Al-Razi says the explanation of faith has already been given in Surat al-Baqara.","anchor":"وقد تقدم القول في الإيمان في سورة البقرة","mentions":["2:"]}]}
